1. Introduction
Distracted driving is one of the important factors affecting road safety (National Center for Statistics and Analysis, 2020). In particular, during autonomous driving, drivers are more susceptible to distraction, and their vigilance to hazardous road stimuli tends to diminish over time (Greenlee et al., 2018). As a result, they may be unable to provide reliable supervision of the vehicle’s interaction with the road environment. To address this issue, the Society of Automotive Engineers (2021), the European Union (2019), and the Chinese government (State Administration for Market Regulation, 2022) have each issued standards and reports emphasizing the importance of monitoring drivers while advanced driving assistance systems are in use. These guidelines aim to assess driver vigilance, ensure the driver’s capacity to drive, and enhance road safety. Distracted driving is the key monitoring content of the EU driver status system. Although the World Health Organization report indicates that 162 countries have enacted legislation prohibiting distracted driving (World Health Organization, 2023), the regulations primarily address physical and visual distractions related to the use of handheld phones, with less focus on cognitive distractions. In fact, it is still unclear which types of cognitive distraction significantly reduce driver vigilance level when advanced driving assistance system functions are used. It is also not clear which type of cognitive distracted driving may help keep drivers at high driver vigilance level in monotonous driving environments. According to resource control theory (Fortenbaugh et al., 2017), in a monotonous driving environment, the executive control exerted by the driver gradually weakens as driving time increases. The theory suggests that internal mind-wandering may represent an individual’s automated processing state. Attention resources are constantly competed for by both mind-wandering and driving tasks, with an increasing proportion being allocated to mind-wandering, which in turn results in a decrease in driving vigilance. Previous studies have predominantly assessed the risk of distracted driving based on a single type of indicator. For example, an improved criterion-based method was employed to analyze the risk level of driving distractions associated with phone use (including voice and video calls) using four eye movement indicators (Wang et al., 2023). However, risk assessments based on a single type of indicator reflect only one aspect of the driver’s vigilance state (such as visual processing) and fail to provide a comprehensive view of overall driver vigilance. In this context, the Multiple Criteria Decision Making (MCDM) method can be applied to evaluate vigilance levels more comprehensively, taking into account multiple types of indicators and the allocation of driver attention. Thus, the objective of this study is to evaluate the decrease in driver vigilance caused by different types of cognitive distraction using the MCDM method. To achieve this, multidimensional data were collected from a series of driving simulator experiments. Three MCDM methods the Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS), Fuzzy Comprehensive Evaluation, and Rank-Sum Ratio (RSR) were applied to evaluate the comprehensive vigilance scores and vigilance levels. This study aims to provide a theoretical basis for limiting distracted driving behaviors in regulations and standards implemented in driver monitoring systems, such as the development of multi-level warnings to prevent the abuse or misuse of advanced driving assistance functions.
2. Literature review
2.1. Driver vigilance and distracted driving
There is currently no widely accepted definition of vigilance. Previous studies on vigilance have primarily focused on drivers’ perception of stimuli and the retention of attention. Mackworth described vigilance as a psychological readiness to perceive and respond (Mackworth, 1948). Davies & Parasuraman, (1982) defined vigilance as a cognitive process related to an individual’s ability to maintain sustained attention while monitoring for signals over an extended period. Based on research utilizing brain imaging techniques, attention has been classified into selective attention, executive attention, and attentional vigilance, the latter of which refers to the process by which individuals maintain concentration on a specific stimulus (Posner & Rothbart, 2007). Distracted driving is a major cause of reduced driver vigilance. When drivers are distracted, their attention shifts to activities unrelated to driving, leading to a decrease in their ability to process driving-related information. The driver distraction guidelines of the U. S. National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) classify distractions into four categories: visual, manual, cognitive, and auditory (National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, 2012). Visual distraction occurs when drivers look away from the road to gather visual information. Manual distraction occurs when drivers take a hand off the steering wheel. Cognitive distraction occurs when drivers divert their mental attention from the driving task. Auditory distraction occurs when drivers pay attention to auditory signals and ignore the road environment. In NHTSA’s cognitive load-related naturalistic driving research, cognitive distraction is categorized into language-generation tasks, language-comprehension tasks, memory tasks and other tasks that impose cognitive load (Angell et al., 2015). Language-generation tasks involve the creation of content, its organization, expression through words, and sequential speech production. Language-comprehension tasks encompass the reception of auditory input, parsing, and understanding, which may also engage long-term and working memory. Memory tasks involve processes such as working memory, retrieval, and occasionally the engagement of executive attention. Other tasks that impose cognitive load include visual-manual tasks.
2.2. Indicators of driving vigilance
Various types of indicators can be used to assess driver vigilance, including not only eye movement indicators but also reaction time, mental workload, and pulse rate.
