As confirmed by Trichle’s study, 83 % of the environmental information received by drivers is dependent on visual perception (Trichler, 1967). Therefore, eye movement is a crucial indicator of driver vigilance, reflecting the efficiency with which visual information is processed. The duration of glances on the road measures the processing of road information from both a temporal and spatial perspective, and this indicator shows performance differences among various types of vigilance decrease. Driving vigilance decreases when drivers engage in visual-manual tasks, leading to a reduction in the duration of glances on the road (Wang et al., 2017). In contrast, auditory-cognitive tasks are associated with longer glances on the road, although they also decrease driving vigilance (Angell et al., 2015). Additionally, fatigue-induced decreases in vigilance are linked to shorter glance durations, accompanied by an increase in PERCLOS (Wang & Xu, 2016). The visual span on the road measures the processing of road information from a spatial perspective, and this indicator also varies in performance across different types of vigilance decrease. A decrease in the visual span on the road has been observed during cognitive tasks when compared to baseline levels (Kountouriotis et al., 2016). Visual span can also be analyzed from both horizontal and vertical perspectives (Robbins & Chapman, 2019). A smaller horizontal and vertical spread of search was observed during cognitive, visual, and visual-cognitive tasks compared to baseline (Liang & Lee, 2010). As the difficulty of a secondary (non-driving related) task increased, the horizontal spread of search increased for visual distraction tasks, while it decreases for cognitive distraction tasks (Son & Park, 2021). Reaction time is a critical indicator of driving vigilance. Previous studies have consistently shown that a decrease in driving vigilance is typically associated with an increase in reaction time, which can be attributed to various factors, including fatigue (Zhang, 2022), distraction (Karthaus et al., 2020), and prolonged monitoring (Greenlee et al., 2018). The Detection Response Task (DRT) has been widely used to measure vigilance response time and hit rate. DRT is also an effective method for assessing the attentional effects of cognitive load imposed on an individual by secondary tasks (Kristina & Jaka, 2018). In accordance with ISO guidelines, sensory stimulation is provided to the driver every 3–5 s, and the driver is required to respond by pressing a button. The duration of sensory stimulus presentation is 1 s. For a response to be considered valid, it must be initiated within 100–2,500 ms after the stimulus is presented (International Organization for Standardization, 2022). The DRT is simple, does not impose additional cognitive load, and is suitable for controlled driving environments, such as driving simulators with variable driving settings. In this study, the DRT was used as a tool to measure vigilance status, reflecting the efficiency of drivers’ response to warning stimuli during distracted tasks. Mental workload refers to the balance between a driver’s limited cognitive resources and the demands of the task at hand (Virtanen et al., 2022). When mental workload exceeds the driver’s cognitive capacity, it can lead to decreased task performance (Wickens, 1984). Therefore, mental workload indicators can be used to assess a driver’s vigilance status. National Aeronautics and Space Administration Task Load Index (NASA-TLX) is a widely used subjective method for evaluating mental workload, encompassing six dimensions: mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort, and frustration level. Additionally, a driver’s pulse rate shows significant variations under different vigilance states, such as awake, drowsy, very sleepy, and fatigued (Shi et al., 2017; Geng et al., 2019). Previous studies have also utilized pulse rate as an indicator to assess driver vigilance (Sun & Yu, 2014; Mukherjee & Roy, 2024).
2.3. Evaluation method
MCDM is a tool for complex decision-making processes that involve both quantitative and qualitative factors. MCDM originated between the 1950 s and 1960 s, and its development accelerated during the 1980 s and 1990 s. Over the past few decades, it has become widely used for evaluating and ranking alternatives in various fields (Zavadskas et al., 2016). Common MCDM methods include TOPSIS, fuzzy comprehensive evaluation, and the RSR method. These three methods and their related research are described as follows. The TOPSIS method, proposed by Hwang & Yoon (1981), is capable of fully utilizing raw data and provides results that accurately reflect the differences between evaluation alternatives. The calculation process of the TOPSIS method is relatively simple. Liang & Zhao (2020) employed the Entropy-TOPSIS method to comprehensively evaluate the operational performance of rail transit in six typical cities. Noughabi et al. (2020) applied the TOPSIS method to evaluate and rank the fatigue factors among heavy vehicle drivers. Yan et al. (2014) used TOPSIS to assess the comprehensive performance of lithium-ion batteries and found that the performance results were consistent with experimental data. However, the accuracy of the TOPSIS method may be compromised by abnormal data, which can lead to unreliable results and make it difficult to account for the weight differences among the indicators. The fuzzy comprehensive evaluation method is employed to conduct an overall evaluation of objects or phenomena constrained by multiple factors, based on the fuzzy set theory proposed by Zadeh (1965). By transforming qualitative evaluations into quantitative ones, this method effectively addresses vague and difficult-to-quantify problems. For instance, Zhu et al., (2022) evaluated coal burst incidents using both the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) and fuzzy comprehensive evaluation. However, the fuzzy comprehensive evaluation models described above rely solely on expert experience to assign indicator weights, which makes them highly susceptible to human biases. To address this limitation, objective weighting methods can be incorporated into the fuzzy comprehensive evaluation model. He et al. (2021) proposed a comprehensive fuzzy evaluation model for driving behavior safety, which combines the Entropy Weight Method (EWM) and AHP to determine the weights objectively, thereby overcoming the subjectivity inherent in fuzzy comprehensive evaluation. However, the calculation process of this method is relatively complex, and it fails to address the issue of information redundancy caused by the correlation between evaluation factors. SRS is a statistical analysis method proposed by Tian (1988), which combines the advantages of classical parametric statistics with those of modern non-parametric statistics. Additionally, the SRS method is less sensitive to outliers, making it a more robust and reliable approach. The RSR method has been widely applied in multi-indicator comprehensive evaluations in areas such as healthcare
