3.3.1. Driving Scene setting
The driving course in the simulator experiment is a 20-km 2-way 6-lane circular highway track with a separation median as shown in Fig. 2. The parameters for the experimental road setup are in accordance with the Design Specification for highway Alignment (Ministry of Transport, 2017). The course consisted of six straight 2-km sections (segment 1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13), two S circular curves with radii of 700 m each (segment 7 and 15), and eight transition curves (segment 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 and 16). To ensure realistic simulated driving conditions and enhanced visual driving experience, a large radius S curve was used to connect the straight sections, and a gentle curve was used to connect the straight sections with the circular curve. The configuration of the straight section is illustrated in Fig. 3. The central median has a width of 3.5 m, the lane width is 3.75 m, the paved shoulder measures 3 m in width, and the unpaved shoulder is 0.75 m wide. The total width of the roadbed is 33.5 m. Lanes are delineated by dashed lines, while solid lines are used to separate the lanes from the shoulder and the left-side curb. The experimental vehicle and the one traveling in front of it were both autonomous vehicles, maintaining a speed of 60 km/h, which is the minimum speed limit on highways. Trees and houses are positioned along both sides of the road.
3.3.2. Road condition observation task
The primary task in which participants were engaged involved observing road conditions. Throughout the formal experiment, participants were instructed to prioritize road condition observation. Although they were not required to control the vehicle, participants were instructed to keep their left hand on the steering wheel at all times during the experiment. The road condition observation task served as the baseline (control) task for comparison with the distraction tasks.
3.3.3. Distraction tasks
This study aims to examine the impact of cognitive distraction tasks on drivers’ levels of vigilance in scenarios where advanced assistance functions are used in real driving environments. In such environments, drivers are required to control the steering wheel and maintain safety monitoring of the road. To closely mimic real-life distracted driving scenarios, this study referenced a previous study on natural driving with mobile phone use, which found that the average duration of distracted driving due to mobile phone use was 69 s (Wang et al., 2020). To ensure that drivers were fully engaged in the distraction tasks, the duration of both the baseline and distraction tasks was set to 90 s. All tasks were conducted on straight road sections and were repeated twice. In this study, distraction tasks were categorized into visual-manual-cognitive and auditory-cognitive tasks based on the driver’s attention resource channels, as shown in .The auditory-cognitive tasks included the 1-back task and a news listening task. The visualmanual-cognitive tasks consisted of a typing and message-sending task, as well as a news-watching task. Following the classification of cognitive load in natural driving research (Angell et al., 2015), cognitive distraction types were further subdivided into language comprehension, language generation, working memory, and combinations of these categories. The N-back task is a cognitive distraction task that involves working memory and decision-making (Stapel et al., 2019). It was used in this study to simulate drivers’ mind-wandering, which affects driving vigilance by competing with road monitoring tasks for the driver’s cognitive resources. Mind-wandering is a common phenomenon during simple tasks, such as road monitoring (Seli, 2016). A 1-back task was used in this study as shown in Fig. 4. Drivers were asked to judge whether the current number that was broadcasted by the simulator was consistent with the previously broadcasted number. For example, when the fourth digit in the sequence is 9, the driver must determine whether the current digit (9) matches the previous digit (9) and respond with either ’yes’ or ’no’. Each digit is presented at a speed of one every two seconds, and a sequence of 45 digits is randomly generated for each trial. These stimulative materials were organized in a pseudo-random order, and two distinct sets of materials (A and B) were used in the experiment. Half of the participants randomly assigned to listen to the AB sequence and the other half listening to the BA sequence. The task of listening to news involves both auditory and cognitive distractions, with the cognitive distraction primarily related to the process of language comprehension. This type of distraction is commonly encountered in activities such as listening to radio broadcasts, audiobooks, and music. In the news listening task, participants are instructed to listen to a voice broadcast of news. A sound prompt signals the beginning and end of the task, and the material consists of two recently published news clips. Participants are randomly assigned to one of two listening orders: half listen in the AB order, while the other half listen in the BA order. The news playback was controlled by the experimenters, and the participants were not involved in any manual distractions. Watching news task involves visual, manual and cognitive distraction, with the cognitive distraction primarily related to the process of language comprehension. Unlike the 1-back task, the cognitive process of this task relies on visual channels and is combined with manual distraction, although the manual distraction setting is relatively simple. The watching news task required participants to
