3.3.4. Detection response task
The Detection Response Task (DRT) task was randomly presented every 3–5 s throughout the experiment. The DRT presentation diagram is shown in Fig. 5. The task consisted of responding to a simultaneous image-sound takeover stimulus that was randomly displayed by an external computer, which was put before the lower left side of the simulator’s screen. White boxes represent that there is no information on the warning interface. Participants were instructed to respond by pressing a button (micro mouse) fixed to the steering wheel with their left thumb when they heard or saw the stimuli. They were also instructed to press the button only once upon hearing or seeing the stimulus, as continuous key pressing could render their response ineffective.
3.3.5. Experimental process
At the beginning of the experiment, participants listened to an explanation of the driving task and the purpose of the study. The total duration of experimental process which consists of six phases was approximately 90 min as shown in Fig. 6. Before starting the first phase of the formal experiment, participants were asked to complete the questionnaires on driver information and driving style, wear the physiological equipment and Tobii Pro Glasses 2 eye tracker, and practice each task. The formal experiment was divided into two phases. Each phase presents all distraction tasks. To overcome the sequential effect, the Latin square experimental design was used to arrange six presentation orders of distraction tasks, with each 10 participants (half male and half female) using two identical sequences. In each phase, participants made one circuit of the course for about 25 min and took a ten-minute break between phases. Participants were asked to complete three types of tasks in the formal experiment: the primary baseline task, four distraction tasks, and the DRT. They were instructed to complete a distraction task by a voice prompt, for example, “Start a watching news task.” After the two formal experiments (ended), the driver filled out a subjective load scale to measure the mental workload of each distraction task and baseline.
3.4. Data collection
To analyze drivers’ vigilance levels during different types of driving distractions, four types of data were collected: 1) the driver’s mental workload as assessed using the NASA-TLX survey questionnaire, 2) DRT task data (reaction time and accuracy), 3) eye movement data (total fixation time on road, vertical spread of search, and horizontal spread of search), and 4) physiological data (blood oxygen and pulse rate). The integration of data was based on the time points recorded by various devices. The driving simulation system marked the start and end time points of each task, and Python code was employed to synchronize these data.
