5. Results
5.1. Evaluation indicators
Evaluation indicators were determined using the random forest model. Eye movement indicators (total fixation time on road, vertical spread of search, and horizontal spread of search), DRT task indicators (reaction time and accuracy), subjective indicators (mental workload), and physiological indicators (blood oxygen and pulse rate) were included in the random forest model as independent variables. The dependent variable is the type of distraction task. Table 2 presents the importance ratio of each feature. Physiological indicators, such as blood oxygen (6.70 %) and pulse rate (8.40 %), which had lower importance, were excluded from the analysis. The remaining six indicators account for more than 84.7 % of the total importance, and analyzing these six indicators is sufficient to validate the rationality of our evaluation model. By removing lowimportance features, the model is simplified, thereby enhancing its interpretability and reducing computational costs. Since lowimportance indicators contribute minimally to the model, their removal has only a marginal impact on model performance.
