6.1. Comparison of distracted tasks in different attention channels
In terms of the attention resource channels involved in distraction, visual and manual cognitive distraction tasks (watching news and sending messages) resulted in lower comprehensive vigilance scores and vigilance levels compared to auditory cognitive distraction tasks (listening to news and 1-back). Specifically, for both the listening to news and watching news tasks, which fall under the same cognitive type (language comprehension), the vigilance scores for the watching news task, which involved both visual and manual distractions, were lower across all three MCDM models compared to the listening to news task. This decrease in vigilance during the watching news task was primarily attributed to its impact on the processing of visual information on the road. As shown in Fig. 8, compared to the listening to news task, the watching news task resulted in a higher proportion of “Very Poor” ratings for drivers’ vertical spread of search and road fixation duration. According to the resource limitation theory, attention resources and capacity are finite (Kahneman, 1973). Therefore, compared to the listening to news task, the watching news task is more likely to compete with road safety monitoring tasks for visual attention resources. Previous studies have shown that visual resources play a critical role in road safety monitoring, as 83 % of the environmental information that drivers receive is visual in nature (Trichler, 1967) This competition for visual attention results in insufficient resources for road allocation, leading to lower vigilance levels.
6.2. Comparison of the visual-manual-cognitive distraction tasks
The results show that, compared to the watching news task, performing the sending information tasks results in a lower level of vigilance, indicating that it has a greater impact on driver vigilance. Both of these tasks fall under the category of visual-manualcognitive distractions. However, while the watching news task only involves language comprehension, the sending information task requires both language comprehension and language generation. Language comprehension and language generation are complex and systematic cognitive processes. Neuroimaging studies have shown that these two processes involve the collaborative activation of three specific brain regions in the left hemisphere (Papathanassiou et al., 2000). Therefore, compared to the watching news task, which involves only language comprehension, the sending messages task, which requires both language comprehension and language generation, demands higher levels of brain activation and attention resources. This increase in brain capacity may limit language support, leading to longer information transmission delays (Josse et al., 2006). Consequently, individuals performing tasks that involve more extensive language processing may experience a higher cognitive workload. The subjective mental workload perceived in the distraction tasks in this study is also consistent with this conclusion. As shown in Fig. 8, compared with the watching news task, the sending messages task exhibited a higher proportion of responses rated as “Very Poor” in terms of mental workload. Previous studies have also observed that as the mental workload of a task increases, individual volunteer performance tends to decrease (Warm et al., 2008).
6.3. Comparison of the auditory-cognitive distraction tasks
In the auditory-cognitive tasks, cognitive distractions related to working memory have a greater impact on vigilance compared to cognitive distractions related to language-comprehension (e.g., the listening to news task). As shown in Fig. 8, the 1-back task showed a higher proportion of “Very Poor” in mental workload compared to the listening news task. Because the 1-back task involved short-term working memory and required drives’ constant comparison and judgment, mental workload of the task was higher compared to the listening news task. The impact of 1-back tasks on vigilance is also reflected in DRT tasks, as indicated by longer DRT response time, where a higher proportion of 1-back tasks had “Very Poor” response time compared to the listening to news task. In the monotonous process of advanced assisted driving, simple auditory-cognitive tasks can help maintain driver vigilance. Among the three MCDM models, the vigilance scores for the listening to news task were similar to those for the no-distraction condition. When drivers perform simple cognitive distraction tasks while using assisted driving functions in monotonous environments, their executive function must continuously allocate resources between driving and the distraction tasks in order to mitigate the resource competition caused by automated mind-wandering (Fortenbaugh et al., 2017). In this context, the stimulus level received by the driver should not be too low. According to Yerkes-Dodson Law, the relationship between attention level and stimulus level can be described by an inverted U-shaped curve (Christianson, 1992), where moderate levels of arousal are associated with improved task performance.
