Similarly, a driving simulator experiment demonstrated that, due to increased arousal, listening to music improved certain aspects of driving performance in a monotonous car-following task (Uenal et al., 2013). A previous study also found that in simple simulated traffic environments, listening to audiobooks while driving led to faster braking responses to hazards (Nowosielski et al., 2018).
6.4. The consistency of the results from the three MCDM methods
The consistency of the results obtained through the three MCDM methods supports the following three conclusions. First, the credibility of the results has been enhanced. Given the differences in data processing and computation across the three methods, this study evaluated driver vigilance during distracted tasks from various perspectives. The consistency of these results highlights the reliability and credibility of the evaluation. Second, it has been verified that the selection of evaluation indicators is stable and reliable, and the data processing and analysis conducted during the evaluation process are scientific and objective. Finally, it is worth to note that the consistency of vigilance performance in distracted tasks is maintained across multiple dimensions or indicators of performance evaluation, allowing different evaluation methods to arrive at consistent conclusions.
6.5. Limitations and future research
This study has several limitations. The first limitation is that it focuses on evaluating drivers’ vigilance levels in cognitive distraction tasks within simple and monotonous driving environments. Consequently, the conclusions primarily apply to the impact of cognitive distraction on vigilance under such monotonous conditions. However, it is important to note that other types of driving environments in the real world—such as high-density urban roads and geometrically complex mountainous roads—were not considered. In these environments, the inherent complexity of driving tasks increases the demand for driver attention. Compared to the simpler and monotonous driving conditions in this study, differences in task execution and attention allocation may arise in more complex environments, which could alter cognitive load and consequently affect vigilance levels. To gain a more comprehensive understanding of the impact of cognitive distraction on vigilance levels in real-world driving conditions, future research should replicate and expand this study to include more complex driving environments. In addition, the participant age range in this study was limited to 20 to 50 years. This primarily reflects the vigilance levels of middle-aged and young drivers when performing cognitive distraction tasks. As a result, the applicability of the study’s conclusions to elderly drivers may be limited. Elderly drivers experience a decline in physical functions, especially attention and reaction time. Their vigilance may differ from that of middle-aged and young drivers. They may also be more susceptible to various factors. To further investigate the impact of cognitive distraction on driver vigilance in different age groups, future research should replicate this study with elderly drivers. This would provide a more comprehensive theoretical foundation for the design of distraction limitation and warning mechanisms for different age groups.
7. Conclusions
This study used a driving simulator to collect multidimensional data on different cognitive distraction tasks and assessed the comprehensive vigilance level of each cognitive distraction task using three multi-criteria decision-making models (fuzzy comprehensive evaluation, TOPSIS, and RSR). The main conclusions were summarized as follows. Regardless of the degree of cognitive load, tasks involving visual-manual-cognitive distraction will significantly reduce the driver’s vigilance level. Therefore, visual-manualcognitive distractions should be limited when drivers use assistance functions. Even in simple and monotonous driving environments, auditory-cognitive distractions involving working memory can lead to a significant decrease in vigilance level. Therefore, it is recommended to warn or limit auditory-cognitive distractions involving working memory. However, in monotonous and simple driving environments, auditory-cognitive distractions involving language comprehension can be allowed to improve driving performance. This study provides theoretical support for limiting distractions by driver vigilance systems, the creation of logical rules for distraction warnings, limiting inherent distractions in the use of advanced driving assistance function, and the institution of related laws and regulations.
